{# Rendered by VisitorDetails::renderVisitorDetails() into the visits log entry for one visit.

   The values were HTML-encoded on the way in: the write path sanitises every tracking parameter
   before storing it, so what the table holds is already encoded. Printing it under
   Twig's autoescape would encode it a second time and show the entities on screen. `rawSafeDecoded`
   is the filter for that: it decodes once and re-encodes exactly once, which is how core's visits log
   templates print a stored label -- see plugins/CustomDimensions/templates/_visitorDetails.twig and
   plugins/Live/templates/_visitorDetails.twig. It also URL-decodes on the way through, so a stored
   value containing a percent sign is not printed verbatim.

   The tooltip comes from the `title` attribute; jQuery UI binds every element that has one. Core
   marks those elements `visitorLogTooltip`, as CustomDimensions does, and following that convention
   is what keeps a later stylesheet or script able to find them. What it must *not* borrow is
   `visitorLogIconWithDetails`: that is Live's icon-strip contract, and its JavaScript reads a nested
   list out of the element, so a line of text under that class shows an empty tooltip on hover.

   Note there is no stored value in an attribute here. Move one into the `title` and it needs
   `|e('html_attr')`, as Live's own icon templates do. #}
<div class="exampleLogTablesAttributes">
    {% if plan %}
        <br/>
        <abbr class="visitorLogTooltip" title="{{ 'ExampleLogTables_StoredByThisPlugin'|translate }}">{{ 'ExampleLogTables_UserPlan'|translate }}:</abbr>
        {{ plan|rawSafeDecoded }}
    {% endif %}
    {% if account %}
        <br/>
        <abbr class="visitorLogTooltip" title="{{ 'ExampleLogTables_StoredByThisPlugin'|translate }}">{{ 'ExampleLogTables_UserAccount'|translate }}:</abbr>
        {{ account|rawSafeDecoded }}
    {% endif %}
</div>
